r fresh visit.\"","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.738,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004006","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"367718d8ff022642a164c8fe44d67873d1856c12f78808b096216ce3113fcd56","compact":"493e9a8033e6cf70750270636a79a3b9485fc0f143e3af0655877238403e5468","nasal":"493e9a8033e6cf70750270636a79a3b9485fc0f143e3af0655877238403e5468"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-7","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":7,"english":"Thereupon, the charioteer told him, \"The sovereign wish to behold you. You have known the occasion; and now decide whether you will go thither or not.”","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.825,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004007","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d74c887cacd9b8ee5e0ab49377b782b62e4aa612ab6896bcbd53bb283dc747d2","compact":"d48ce460d534deadf1250ba27c127685b142dc06df5c32d9abcbbe311263f204","nasal":"d48ce460d534deadf1250ba27c127685b142dc06df5c32d9abcbbe311263f204"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-8","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":8,"english":"Hearing the charioteer's speech, Rāma also in haste repaired to the king's palace, with the view of again beholding the lord of Man. And on hearing of Rāma's arrival, king Daſaratha inade him enter his own chamber, with the view of communicating to him something exceedingly agreeable.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.887,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004008","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"24107c3035780ce096eb4041170650dd30dd249413545e6388ba26710b7f0a37","compact":"6dc6cbc39b0edc73f009565190bb201965a43ea76e6757c10729d1a17dca6c34","nasal":"6dc6cbc39b0edc73f009565190bb201965a43ea76e6757c10729d1a17dca6c34"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-9","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":9,"english":"Hearing the charioteer's speech, Rāma also in haste repaired to the king's palace, with the view of again beholding the lord of Man. And on hearing of Rāma's arrival, king Daſaratha inade him enter his own chamber, with the view of communicating to him something exceedingly agreeable.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.892,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004009","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b4d491dfb53cbf2098102bcf7978cf248df7a6dd3123fd4e8971d2026a8a4eda","compact":"dce7ba923688f4608d233bda00dca8863a1811834aee70d1afd5375c22d8e183","nasal":"dce7ba923688f4608d233bda00dca8863a1811834aee70d1afd5375c22d8e183"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-10","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":10,"english":"And immediately on entering his father's residence, the graceful Rāghava seeing his father from a distance, bent low with clasped hands.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004010","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"28485cf2bc0ef522985bac4137035ebdcb7a43d5e5530257c09aa5e22f261907","compact":"962ce99eeeb7e99ee1e7760c4cd41ca21798d9a751b34d5e1bd4f838aaa2e22b","nasal":"fbc98c15aa0b950224896fa01d184355d18c069ed15b07fb12e5d106de14fe0c"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-11","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":11,"english":"Thereupon raising Räma as he was bending down, and embracing him, and pointing out a seat, the protector of the earth again spoke to him.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.422,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004011","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"416bcf4dad8ef529296d2ad4f620fa33a71c62cfde34666068a8a25ca1ff75f3","compact":"496c8f101f622fdb64b6501f0cee40efa2d90eac21034b9643c9c4c936977b20","nasal":"161395fb7463e80563a10bada0500a7b6db1be876e449720a14b629f189c0207"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-12","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":12,"english":"O Ráma, enjoying at my will the good things of life, I have grown old; and have attained great age. I have worshipped the deities by celebrating hundreds of sacrifices with numerous Dakşiņās and gifts of boiled rice.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.747,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004012","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a51895f63c415661bb5a2b28032afb0c95fef3515b681a8b320c926be6c97c9b","compact":"ae3e1f20b89d278d4d186200a3ad2f4907f4838c3e51581a005045ab27302623","nasal":"beddd0bd068ea2bb1ee27c3a47a2c49c1b01830d9566ce4c5b1c569f104de033"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-13","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":13,"english":"And incomparable on earth, you have been born to me for a son after my heart. I have given whatever had been wanted (by others); I have finished my studies.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004013","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b05719b5aaddebb81a39a666aef306081e700b7219926c7e80fc375e237a2f70","compact":"4e30aacaeedee3f91b236a1a5acc9785f7737164575b659168dc51327565b117","nasal":"4e30aacaeedee3f91b236a1a5acc9785f7737164575b659168dc51327565b117"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-14","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":14,"english":"O foremost of men. I have, O hero, acted and enjoyed. I have been emancipated from my obligations to the celestials and saints, and the Pitris, and the Vipras, and myself.* *Sacrifice, study, son, gift, enjoyment are the five means of, clearing the debts one owes to the celestials, the saints, the pitrs, the vipras and self.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.847,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004014","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"36d9588677aa120efca8881771d292204652436c4c4f316f36a3b1f7bda28e18","compact":"61e73165ee26dc05c8d34b0cf969ad23b782435753a993c2175a5691dbffb1fb","nasal":"61e73165ee26dc05c8d34b0cf969ad23b782435753a993c2175a5691dbffb1fb"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-15","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":15,"english":"And naught now remain to be done by me save the installation. Therefore it behoves you to do even what I say to you. To-day the subjects in a body have expressed their desire of having you for their sovereign. Therefore, O son, I shall install you as the heir-apparent. O Rāghava, tonight I have dreamt inauspicious dreams. Stars with tremendous sounds, shoot by day, accompanied with thunder-claps.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004015","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7f8056b2ce944726d931d7069a0a3545d20a61ade45f66bbf01dc7670ad5bbf8","compact":"5c74c9e8c3f1d1cc82f798fd70cbdcd4f902ea1ef3ef6ecce289fc87f3317d00","nasal":"5c74c9e8c3f1d1cc82f798fd70cbdcd4f902ea1ef3ef6ecce289fc87f3317d00"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-16","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":16,"english":"And naught now remain to be done by me save the installation. Therefore it behoves you to do even what I say to you. To-day the subjects in a body have expressed their desire of having you for their sovereign. Therefore, O son, I shall install you as the heir-apparent. O Rāghava, tonight I have dreamt inauspicious dreams. Stars with tremendous sounds, shoot by day, accompanied with thunder-claps.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004016","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3fa0f7c9e627cb0b0d05d01f051a5c4a3beadda293bdcf7f8c71965802ec1293","compact":"40b4f7e9cd643d76b3b07aee99471cefea2aa1a48397ca8039ebad53021ed5df","nasal":"b1c008c95e5d822729b90b61e1f26b023549308f4868dd2bdfd53c13d9218493"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-17","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":17,"english":"And naught now remain to be done by me save the installation. Therefore it behoves you to do even what I say to you. To-day the subjects in a body have expressed their desire of having you for their sovereign. Therefore, O son, I shall install you as the heir-apparent. O Rāghava, tonight I have dreamt inauspicious dreams. Stars with tremendous sounds, shoot by day, accompanied with thunder-claps.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.529,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004017","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"181b6f3d6c9f8f272aceecd2f314fd4dae7d3d7897a40e704399178aff755d43","compact":"102fd285caddde1af1b747e5112886f67224ad1dbe0dfacb248e09feb9266d61","nasal":"d064a1714e9752745c5a140cdd29ae5c918f8936fb750aa68330176bad3d482d"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-18","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":18,"english":"The astrologers say that the star of my life has been invaded by those terrible planets, the Sun, Mars, and Rāhu.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.848,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004018","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"71572aa9beccb1c684c08b734c5070d92da95f5d7a8d4ef7c89f4e8462009cf6","compact":"aa9c3531ba6a601167399502f1f586ad4f738fec743956fb79c11b5d866601f2","nasal":"7245f73f5b6fa8673ea7217f4282f2bb41e7a1be9da4dc0426e66d0014b9319f"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-19","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":19,"english":"It generally happens that when such signs manifest themselves, the king comes by a terrible calamity, and may meet with death itself.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.6,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004019","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"50d43982fd8d5d1f728cb508bcbd0c9cfc62917ca674f8390333f8ba37bb6539","compact":"456ca19968448230ee2e90ef31e94e3b7761e4296a656c6ceab6fdf016d66f6c","nasal":"456ca19968448230ee2e90ef31e94e3b7761e4296a656c6ceab6fdf016d66f6c"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-20","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":20,"english":"Therefore, O Raghava, my thoughts change, be you installed (in the kingdom), for fickle is the mind of all creatures.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004020","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1d52935d2e344aa330a1c742ac5d62fa5912309d6e6da39251df6b4f2f04cd3c","compact":"aa8c6b4d71e2ddf9a0f1e8df49ec2bcdf6cc314dea7e91165130bd240b7d24d6","nasal":"9040339208a707a9b366ceb4dd0ef2fdc5ffd37ae902b61f5a6e1f02b4655d5f"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-21","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":21,"english":"To-day, before meeting Pusya, the moon has entered the Punarvasu asterism; and the astrologers say that to-morrow it will certainly be in conjunction with Pușya.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.88,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-17"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004021","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"96bffcc33cc090b6c5cdfd21880c06023268e7ae05186609f9b4ba97bc6452fc","compact":"995af68feed48ca99b568222cab6189bd4028aaad1d3a657493d1b74d65147ff","nasal":"ed835159a8adc33f2cb595310756ee4a2799b37566e57f1a72327554c468eae2"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-22","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":22,"english":"My heart urges me to install you during the Pusya conjunction, so Oh! afflicter of foes, I shall install you to-morrow as heir-apparent.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004022","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0acbff3af7a61879289a7473fd7f8db7f09441b40fd9358f3195700cc5ebad49","compact":"c612f3ac19f60f4453d85ebd05e825acf0801eddc509eaabdc7e5e2284e475b9","nasal":"f75f76074cf8e42e1906fcef7fabc1400fdda4c352baad66417c6edf0b479381"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-23","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":23,"english":"Therefore do you along with my daughter-inlaw, commencing from sun-set, serving the prescribed restrictions, and lying down on a bed of Kusa grass, spend the night in fast.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.781,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004023","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"10a4a160535bac5c066c37a8c6b1c0565f926e3c94f3983882ba04dd8408177a","compact":"25939daccbdee40792cf4a28bb91ee8fc478828ff07dad18f07ab3a9e68afeb7","nasal":"25939daccbdee40792cf4a28bb91ee8fc478828ff07dad18f07ab3a9e68afeb7"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-24","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":24,"english":"And let your friends vigilantly protect you all around, for many are the impediments that happen in affairs like this.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004024","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3a277f74a2bb6e30891a04781972183b18e4b0fe647492d95505a20f60dd400f","compact":"8ba474b6e8995a296dfe4bdfbbb2a85b75cd1eaf1788f5ef65841a1177093e6b","nasal":"ba1318ff7e353ac2359a6d905374193452a8014308ee50c9452f40ebd45d37a0"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-25","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":25,"english":"In my opinion, during the interval that Bharata is away from the city, should your installation be effected most opportunity.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004025","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e0c65149af3c7e52354487a1455ba93527449b51c8a2bf4da409327dcd561995","compact":"8ee65e320c487854303c3be83d74e3e69b6764006fe647c1418e59ad594dcfae","nasal":"8ee65e320c487854303c3be83d74e3e69b6764006fe647c1418e59ad594dcfae"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-4-26","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":4,"verse":26,"english":"Even though your brother Bharata ever stayed entirely by the course of the honest; he followed his elder brother; and is righteous-souled; tenderhearted; and of subdued senses. But in my opinion, the hearts of men are inconstant, and, O Rāghava, the hearts even of the virtuous change by the action of the natural impulses.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.946,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-4-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2004026","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a95cd8de3c4d7e65236cfac5435d45c69dc